{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I understand the current structure. The task5 is to refactor `internal/prompt` into a builder trio:\n- `internal/builder/templates` (go embed of templates)\n- `internal/builder/pibuilder` (pi unified entry)\n- `internal/builder/xxxxbuilder` (extension point, RuntimeBuilder interface)\n\nBut wait, the task says the test command is `go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/...`. So after refactoring, there should be an `internal/builder` package. And there's a `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")` method expected.\n\nThe test methods:\n1. 正常路径: `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick && go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/... -v` — build success, tests green.\n2. 边界: `git diff --stat internal/prompt/templates/` no diff; `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -cE 'plan/task|doing/debug|/jobs/|/domain'` ≥ 1.\n3. 异常: `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")` returns error containing `requirement cannot be empty`.\n\nI need to write a Python test script that:\n1. Builds the project (go build) and runs go tests.\n2. Checks `git diff --stat internal/prompt/templates/` is empty.\n3. Runs `./bin/rick plan --dry-run` and greps for the path patterns.\n4. Tests `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")` returns error. But this is a Go-level test — how would the Python script test a Go function? \n\nHmm. The Python script can't directly call Go code. The way to test `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")` from Python is to either:\n- Run a `go test` that contains a specific test for that case, OR\n- Use a small Go program.\n\nActually, looking at the test method more carefully:\n\n\"异常（builder 缺参数）：前置条件 = 重构完成；输入 = `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")`（requirement 为空字符串，其余参数为空）；操作 = 调用检查 error；预期 = 返回 error 含 `requirement cannot be empty`。\"\n\nThis is a Go-level unit test. The Python test script needs to verify this via `go test`. The best approach is to run `go test ./internal/builder/...` and check that the test passes. But to specifically test the `BuildPlan(\"\")` case, I could write a small Go program that imports the builder package and calls `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")`, OR rely on a unit test that exists in the builder package.\n\nActually, since this is the RED phase (test generation), I should write a test script that FAILS now because:\n1. `internal/builder` doesn't exist yet → `go build` / `go test ./internal/builder/...` will fail.\n\nBut I should write it in a way that will pass once the refactoring is done.\n\nLet me think about how to test `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")` from Python. The cleanest way that doesn't pollute the repo is to write a temporary Go test file in the builder package... but that would modify the source. Alternatively, use `go run` with a temporary main.go in a temp directory that imports the module. But `go run` with an external file importing the module is tricky.\n\nActually, a simpler approach: use `go test` with `-run` to run a specific existing test. But we don't know the test name in advance.\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. The task says \"测试方法\" gives three test cases. The test script must implement all of them. For the \"异常\" case, I need to verify that `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")` returns an error containing `requirement cannot be empty`.\n\nApproach options:\n1. Write a temporary Go file `internal/builder/zz_pibuilder_emptytest_test.go` that tests this, run `go test`, then remove it. This is intrusive and leaves files if interrupted.\n2. Create a temporary standalone Go program that imports the package via the module path and calls it. Since this is a module (`github.com/sunquan/rick`), I can create a temp dir inside the module (e.g., a temp package under the module), write a `main.go` with `go run`, then clean up. Actually simpler: use `go run` with a temp file in a subdirectory of the module.\n\nActually the simplest robust approach for the Python script is:\n\n```python\nimport tempfile, subprocess, os\n\n# Create a temp Go program inside the module\ntmpdir = tempfile.mkdtemp(dir=project_root)  # must be inside module for import to work\n```\n\nWait, actually `go run` needs the file to be in a package directory within the module. Let me think: if I create `project_root/.tmp_emptytest/main.go` with `package main` that imports `github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/builder` and calls `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")`, then `go run ./.tmp_emptytest` should work, and I can clean up after.\n\nBut wait — is `PIBuilder` the right type name? The task says `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")`. Let me look at the spec (task2) to understand the expected API. The task references spec (task2) KR2.3 and RFC §4.2「builder 三件」. Let me check the spec and RFC to understand the exact expected API names.\n\nLet me search for the spec and RFC."}}
