entDef struct' not in src and 'type AgentDef ' not in src: ...\n```\n\nHmm, `Method` and `AgentDef` might be `type Method struct {...}` or `type AgentDef struct {...}`. I'll check `type Method struct` and `type AgentDef struct` (most likely). Actually, to be safe, check `Method` and `AgentDef` appear as types. Let me check `type Method` and `type AgentDef`.\n\nWait, actually re-reading KR2: \"`Method`/`AgentDef` 类型在 pibuilder 落地时定义\". So `type Method` and `type AgentDef` should be defined. I'll check `type Method` and `type AgentDef` (substring).\n\nActually, let me reconsider — `BuildAgents(method []Method) ([]AgentDef, error)` already references `Method` and `AgentDef` in the interface signature, which forces them to be defined (else compile error). Since `go build`/`go test` must pass, if the interface signature has `[]Method` and `[]AgentDef`, those types MUST exist. So checking the interface signature is sufficient; I don't need a separate check for `type Method`/`type AgentDef`. But adding them is harmless. I'll include them but leniently.\n\nLet me now also think about whether `internal/builder` might be structured as subpackages (`internal/builder/pibuilder`, `internal/builder/xxxxbuilder`). The task says \"新增 `xxxxbuilder.go`\" (a file) and \"`Method`/`AgentDef` 类型在 pibuilder 落地时定义\" and test command `go test ./internal/builder/...`. This suggests a flat package `internal/builder` with files `pibuilder.go`, `xxxxbuilder.go`, etc. The `...` in `go test ./internal/builder/...` is just the standard way to include the package (and any subpackages).\n\nI'll write the structural checks against `internal/builder/*.go` (flat). If the implementation uses subpackages, my checks would fail — but that would indicate the implementation deviated from the task5.md spec (which says \"新增 xxxxbuilder.go\" in the builder package). So flat checks are correct.\n\nActually, wait, let me reconsider. \"pibuilder（pi 统一入口，组合 plan/doing/easy/human-loop 子 builder）\". \"子 builder\" = s